::ENG3_U06_L01_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: Which presentation focus is narrow and clear? {
~My presentation is about school, books, speeches, and technology.#Incorrect. This focus is too broad.
=Credible evidence helps listeners evaluate a speaker's claim.#Correct. This focus is specific and communication-centered.
~I will say many things about a topic.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~Evidence exists in lots of places.#Incorrect. This is too general.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Draft order: closing, evidence, focus, explanation. Which order is clearest? {
~Closing, focus, evidence, explanation.#Incorrect. The closing should not begin the presentation.
~Evidence, focus, explanation, closing.#Incorrect. Evidence before focus can confuse listeners.
~Explanation, focus, evidence, closing.#Incorrect. Explanation needs the focus and evidence first.
=Focus, evidence, explanation, closing.#Correct. This gives listeners a logical sequence.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.R.10] Source sentence: The article explains that paraphrasing evidence can make complex information easier for listeners to understand. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
=The article says putting evidence in your own words can help listeners understand difficult information.#Correct. This preserves the meaning.
~The article says listeners should never hear evidence.#Incorrect. This reverses the meaning.
~The article says paraphrasing makes information more confusing.#Incorrect. This is the opposite.
~The article says speakers should copy every source exactly.#Incorrect. The source supports paraphrasing.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Context: Which phrase is the best academic replacement for "facts people can trust"? {
~good stuff#Incorrect. This is informal and vague.
~random details#Incorrect. Random details are not necessarily trustworthy.
=credible evidence#Correct. This is precise academic vocabulary.
~cool information#Incorrect. This is informal.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: Which evidence best supports a presentation about the value of revision? {
~A claim that revision is boring.#Incorrect. This does not support the value of revision.
=Data showing revised essays had clearer claims than first drafts.#Correct. This is relevant and credible.
~A personal guess that revision might work.#Incorrect. A guess is weak evidence.
~An unrelated statistic about lunch menus.#Incorrect. This does not support the focus.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q06::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Draft: The speaker present credible evidence clearly. Which revision is correct? {
=The speaker presents credible evidence clearly.#Correct. The singular subject needs "presents."
~The speaker present credible evidence clearly.#Incorrect. Subject and verb do not agree.
~The speaker presenting credible evidence clearly.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
~The speaker presentation credible evidence clearly.#Incorrect. "Presentation" is not the correct verb.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q07::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: A listener says, "I heard several examples, but I never understood the main point." What should the speaker improve? {
~Add more unrelated examples.#Incorrect. More examples will not fix missing focus.
~Remove all evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence is still needed.
~Speak faster.#Incorrect. Speed does not fix focus.
=State a clear focus before giving evidence.#Correct. The audience needs the main point first.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q08::[MLA.ENG3.R.10] Source sentence: The survey found that students remembered presentations better when speakers organized ideas in clear sections. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~Students remembered presentations less when organization was clear.#Incorrect. This reverses the finding.
~Speakers should avoid sections during presentations.#Incorrect. The source supports clear sections.
=The survey showed that clear sections helped students remember presentations.#Correct. This keeps the meaning.
~The survey found that organization never matters.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q09::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: Which opening best prepares listeners for an organized presentation? {
~I have a lot of things to say and will start somewhere.#Incorrect. This lacks focus.
=Today I will explain two ways credible evidence strengthens oral presentations.#Correct. This previews focus and organization.
~This topic is about many different ideas.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~I did not organize this, but here it goes.#Incorrect. This weakens audience confidence.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q10::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Context: Which sentence uses academic vocabulary appropriately in a presentation? {
=The source strengthens my claim because it provides credible evidence.#Correct. "Strengthens," "claim," and "credible evidence" are academic and accurate.
~The source makes my stuff sound good.#Incorrect. This is informal.
~The source does a thing to my idea.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~The source is a very source-like source.#Incorrect. This is circular.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q11::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: Which presentation point is most clearly connected to the focus "credible evidence builds audience trust"? {
~My favorite speech was short.#Incorrect. This does not connect to credible evidence.
~Some presentations use slides.#Incorrect. This may be true but is not directly connected.
=Listeners are more likely to trust a claim when the speaker explains where the evidence comes from.#Correct. This point directly supports the focus.
~Public speaking can happen in many rooms.#Incorrect. This is unrelated.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q12::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Draft: The presentation has a focus, it needs stronger evidence. Which revision is correct? {
~The presentation has a focus it needs stronger evidence.#Incorrect. This is a run-on.
=The presentation has a focus, but it needs stronger evidence.#Correct. The comma and conjunction correctly join the ideas.
~The presentation, has a focus, it needs stronger evidence.#Incorrect. The punctuation is incorrect.
~The presentation has, a focus but, it needs stronger evidence.#Incorrect. The commas are misplaced.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q13::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: Why should a speaker paraphrase complex evidence? {
~To hide the source from listeners.#Incorrect. Credibility requires clear source use.
~To change the evidence's meaning.#Incorrect. A paraphrase should preserve meaning.
~To avoid explaining the claim.#Incorrect. Explanation is still needed.
=To make the evidence easier for listeners to understand.#Correct. Oral delivery benefits from clear paraphrase.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q14::[MLA.ENG3.R.10] Source sentence: Credible sources give audiences a reason to trust a speaker's claims. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~Credible sources make audiences distrust every claim.#Incorrect. This reverses the meaning.
=Trustworthy sources help audiences believe a speaker's claims.#Correct. This keeps the meaning.
~Speakers should not use sources.#Incorrect. The source supports credible sources.
~Claims are stronger when sources are missing.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q15::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: Which closing best returns to the presentation focus? {
~That is all I have, and I am done.#Incorrect. This does not reinforce the focus.
~I also like many unrelated things.#Incorrect. This distracts from the focus.
=Credible evidence does more than fill time; it gives listeners a reason to trust the message.#Correct. This closing returns to the focus.
~I forgot my focus, but thanks.#Incorrect. This is not effective.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q16::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Context: Which phrase best names the main message of a presentation? {
=central claim#Correct. This is appropriate academic vocabulary.
~random stuff#Incorrect. This is informal.
~talking thing#Incorrect. This is vague.
~extra noise#Incorrect. This does not mean main message.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q17::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: A presentation has a clear focus but evidence from an unknown website with no author or date. What is the concern? {
=The evidence may not be credible.#Correct. Unknown authorship and date can weaken credibility.
~The focus is automatically wrong.#Incorrect. The concern is evidence credibility.
~The speaker should remove all organization.#Incorrect. Organization is still needed.
~The evidence is stronger because it lacks a source.#Incorrect. Missing source information weakens trust.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q18::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Draft: Before the speaker explains the evidence. The audience needs the focus. Which revision is correct? {
~Before the speaker explains the evidence.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
=Before the speaker explains the evidence, the audience needs the focus.#Correct. The dependent clause is joined to a complete sentence.
~The audience needs the focus before.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
~Before, the speaker explains, the evidence, the audience.#Incorrect. The punctuation disrupts meaning.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q19::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: Which outline is most logical for an oral presentation? {
~Evidence, evidence, unrelated story, focus.#Incorrect. The focus comes too late and the story is unrelated.
=Focus, point 1, evidence 1, point 2, evidence 2, closing.#Correct. This sequence is organized for listeners.
~Closing, evidence, point 2, focus.#Incorrect. The order is confusing.
~Unrelated story, unrelated statistic, focus, no closing.#Incorrect. This is not coherent.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q20::[MLA.ENG3.R.10] Source sentence: The report notes that listeners benefit when speakers explain evidence instead of listing facts. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
=The report says audiences understand better when speakers explain what evidence means.#Correct. This preserves the meaning.
~The report says speakers should only list facts.#Incorrect. This reverses the meaning.
~The report says evidence should never be explained.#Incorrect. The source says explanation helps.
~The report says listeners do not benefit from presentations.#Incorrect. This misstates the source.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q21::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: What makes evidence relevant in an oral presentation? {
=It directly supports the presentation focus.#Correct. Relevant evidence connects to the focus.
~It is the longest detail available.#Incorrect. Length does not make evidence relevant.
~It is unrelated but interesting.#Incorrect. Interesting evidence can still be irrelevant.
~It appears after the closing.#Incorrect. Placement after closing weakens organization.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q22::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Context: Which sentence uses "coherent focus" correctly? {
~The coherent focus was a pencil on the desk.#Incorrect. This treats the phrase as a physical object.
~The speaker coherent focus the evidence.#Incorrect. This is not standard English.
~The focus coherent was many unrelated topics.#Incorrect. This is not clear or correct.
=The presentation had a coherent focus because every point supported the same message.#Correct. This uses the phrase accurately.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q23::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: A speaker includes credible evidence but never explains it. What is the best revision? {
~Add more unexplained evidence.#Incorrect. That repeats the problem.
~Remove the focus statement.#Incorrect. The focus is needed.
=Explain how each piece of evidence supports the focus.#Correct. Evidence needs explanation.
~End the presentation immediately after the evidence.#Incorrect. This leaves listeners without meaning.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q24::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Draft: The evidence is credible and it supports the focus. Which version is correctly punctuated? {
~The evidence, is credible and it supports the focus.#Incorrect. The comma after evidence is incorrect.
=The evidence is credible, and it supports the focus.#Correct. The comma before "and" joins two independent clauses.
~The evidence is credible and, it supports the focus.#Incorrect. The comma is misplaced.
~The evidence is credible, and, it supports the focus.#Incorrect. The comma after "and" is unnecessary.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q25::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: Which question should a speaker ask while reviewing an outline? {
=Does each point support my focus with credible evidence?#Correct. This checks focus, organization, and evidence.
~Can I add unrelated details to make it longer?#Incorrect. Unrelated details weaken focus.
~Can I hide my evidence from the audience?#Incorrect. Evidence should be clear.
~Can I skip the closing completely?#Incorrect. A closing helps the presentation feel complete.
}
